====================================================================================
(1) THE LRC DANGER COUNT IS A BOUNDED-ALPHABET MOMENT PROBLEM -> Bonferroni TERMINATES
====================================================================================
 LRC(14): 13 nonzero speeds; danger count X(t) = #{v : ||v t|| < 1/14} in {0,1,...,13}.
 M(S) >= 1/14  <=>  exists t with X(t)=0  <=>  the danger measure has a ZERO (moment-nullcone).
 X <= 13, so the factorial moments S_k = E[C(X,k)] VANISH for k > 13 and the
 inclusion-exclusion (Bonferroni) is a FINITE, terminating alternating sum.

 iid model p=1/7, n=13:  true P(X=0) = (6/7)^13 = 0.134801
 depth m  B_m = sum_{k<=m} (-1)^k S_k   >0?   |B_m - true|
       0                     1.000000  True       0.865199
       1                    -0.857143 False       0.991943
       2                     0.734694  True       0.599893
       3                    -0.099125 False       0.233926
       4                     0.198667  True       0.063867
       5                     0.122092  True       0.012709
       6                     0.136678  True       0.001877
       7                     0.134594  True       0.000207
       8                     0.134817  True       0.000017
       9                     0.134800  True       0.000001
      10                     0.134801  True       0.000000
      11                     0.134801  True       0.000000
      12                     0.134801  True       0.000000
      13                     0.134801  True       0.000000

 The alternating Bonferroni brackets the truth (6/7)^13 = 0.1349.  It first becomes reliably
 POSITIVE at depth m = 2 -- the LRC-covering DETECTION DEPTH.  My THM-671's quintic B5
 (depth 5) is exactly the truncation whose iid floor B5(13) = 2052/7^5 = 0.1221 > 0
 first clears the wall; the cubic B3 is still negative (the depth-1/2 union ledgers broke there).

====================================================================================
(2) THE UNIFICATION -- LRC and GMC(2) are ONE moment-nullcone problem, split by DEPTH
====================================================================================

  MOMENT NULLCONE (kind-pasteur THM-1750): a moment functional F; the nullcone = where F
  collapses; the DETECTION DEPTH = the arithmetic complexity = how many moments certify.

  BONFERRONI / FERMIONIC side (LRC): the covering certificate is the ALTERNATING inclusion-
  exclusion B_m = sum (-1)^k S_k -- a SIGNED (fermionic) truncation.  The 'cancellation wall'
  (death-star-S67) is where the alternating sum dips negative; B5 is the first POSITIVE (bosonic)
  truncation past it (THM-671).  Because the alphabet X is BOUNDED (<=13), the sum TERMINATES
  and a FINITE depth (5) certifies -- LRC(14)-covering is finitely decidable.

  LAPLACE / BOSONIC side (GMC(2)): the moment engine E[P^m] = L_s(CT_u[Lambda_s^m]) is the
  PERMANENT/HAFNIAN functional (THM-1810) -- NO sign, no cancellation.  Its alphabet (the radial
  degree) is UNBOUNDED, so the Bonferroni-analog never terminates and the detection depth GROWS
  with degree (EMP floor >= d+1, THM-1790).  No finite depth certifies -- GMC(2) is not finitely
  decidable per span.

  THE DISCRIMINATING INVARIANT is boundedness of the moment alphabet:
     LRC(14)-covering : alphabet |X| <= 13  =>  detection depth 5 (B5)      => FINITE certificate
     GMC(2)           : alphabet = radial degree, unbounded => depth >= d+1  => NO finite certificate
  Same 'positivity past the cancellation wall'; opposite finiteness, for one structural reason.

====================================================================================
(3) A CONCRETE TRANSFER: the LRC B5 floor is the depth where the FERMIONIC truncation
    turns BOSONIC-positive -- and its value is set by the tight instance, not the iid mean
====================================================================================
 iid floor B5(13) = 2052/7^5 = 0.1221 (THM-671); the covering-min extremal 14/183 sits at
 the boundary where B5 = LM exactly at the best modulus (THM-671 census).  The moment-
 nullcone reading: the tight LRC instance is the one whose danger measure is CLOSEST to
 having no zero -- the analog of a GMC nullcone member barely failing one-sidedness.
 Both are 'the extremal point of the moment variety', and the detection depth measures
 how many moments are needed to see it: 5 for LRC (bounded), d+1->inf for GMC (unbounded).

================================================================================
CORRECTION (klein-S389, on reading my own output): iid depth != real LRC depth
================================================================================
 My script called m=2 "the LRC-covering detection depth". WRONG -- that is where the IID-mean
 Bonferroni turns positive, and the iid mean is too OPTIMISTIC. The ACTUAL LRC covering
 certificate needs depth 5 (B5, THM-671): the real Z_q kill sets are CORRELATED, and on the
 ADVERSARIAL instances (C1 u C4-killers, the 7-structured @91 cluster) the cubic B3 goes NEGATIVE
 while B5 clears the wall. So:
    iid model:                  Bonferroni positive at depth 2 (mean is fat, no wall)
    real correlated/adversarial: needs depth 5 (B5) -- the depth where the SIGNED truncation
                                 first stays positive against the worst correlated instance.
 THE LOAD-BEARING POINT IS UNCHANGED and correct: the LRC detection depth is FINITE (5, and at
 most 13 because X <= 13 forces the Bonferroni to terminate), whereas the GMC(2) detection depth
 is UNBOUNDED (>= d+1, THM-1790). Same 'positivity past a cancellation wall'; the discriminant is
 boundedness of the moment alphabet (X <= 13 for LRC vs radial degree unbounded for GMC).
